@startuml seq-hand-admin
title Hand admin plane — one shared token for every hand (F3)

participant "serve" as serve
participant "atenet (Envoy)\nHost-header routing" as net
participant "hand h-<sid>" as hand
participant "another session's hand\nh-<other>" as other

serve -> net : POST /admin/identity  {session, actor}
net -> hand : Host: h-<sid>.agents…
serve -> net : POST /admin/upstreams {mcp servers + headers}
net -> hand : (federates user's MCP servers, with credentials)
serve -> net : POST /admin/grant {grant, names}
net -> hand : (hand then pulls values — see seq-vault-grant)
hand -> serve : GET /v1/hand/admin-verify (Bearer <grant>)
serve -> hand : {session: sess-<sid>}
hand -> hand : adminAuthed(): grant valid AND session == own latched identity

note over hand, other #E4F7E4
  <b>FIXED (F3, F14).</b> /admin takes a <b>session-scoped HMAC grant</b> with a
  2-minute TTL, verified against serve and bound to this hand's own identity.
  HAND_ADMIN_TOKEN is <b>no longer mounted into the hand at all</b>.
  Verified live: legacy token -> 401 (was 200); a forged grant naming
  another session -> 401.

  Model-written code also no longer runs in this container: commands and
  filesystem work happen in a second <b>executor</b> container with its own
  rootfs, its own PID namespace and no credentials (F14).

  Remaining: the binding is serve's claim compared to a Host-derived name,
  not cryptographic actor identity. <b>ActorIdentity mTLS is the endgame.</b>
  Egress is still unrestricted (F9), so reachability is not the control —
  having nothing worth stealing is.
end note
hand -[#2E7D32]-> other : lateral movement (a grant for h-<sid> is refused by h-<other>)
@enduml
